.\" feof.3 - check and reset stream status
.TH FEOF 3 "May 2026" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
feof, ferror, clearerr \- check and reset stream status
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "int feof(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "int ferror(FILE *" stream ");"
.PP
.BI "void clearerr(FILE *" stream ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR feof ()
function tests the end-of-file indicator for the stream pointed to by
.IR stream ,
returning a non-zero value if it is set.
The end-of-file indicator can be cleared only by an explicit call to
.BR clearerr ().
.PP
The end-of-file indicator is set when a read from the underlying file
descriptor returns zero bytes, signalling that no more input is
available.
A successful call to
.BR ungetc (3)
also clears the end-of-file indicator for the stream.
.PP
The
.BR ferror ()
function tests the error indicator for the stream pointed to by
.IR stream ,
returning a non-zero value if it is set.
The error indicator is set when an underlying
.BR read (2)
or
.BR write (2)
system call returns -1, indicating that an I/O error has occurred on the
stream.
The error indicator can be reset only by an explicit call to
.BR clearerr ().
.PP
The
.BR clearerr ()
function clears both the end-of-file and error indicators for the stream
pointed to by
.IR stream .
.PP
The end-of-file and error indicators are distinct: a stream may report
end-of-file without an error, or an error without end-of-file, and a
caller that needs to distinguish the two conditions after a short or
failed I/O operation should test both.
.SH RETURN VALUE
The
.BR feof ()
function returns non-zero if the end-of-file indicator is set for
.IR stream ,
and zero otherwise.
.PP
The
.BR ferror ()
function returns non-zero if the error indicator is set for
.IR stream ,
and zero otherwise.
.PP
The
.BR clearerr ()
function returns no value.
.PP
These functions do not themselves set
.IR errno .
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program reads a file to its end, then distinguishes
end-of-file from a read error.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/etc/hostname", "r");
    int c;

    if (fp == NULL)
        return 1;
    while ((c = fgetc(fp)) != EOF)
        putchar(c);
    if (ferror(fp))
        fputs("read error\en", stderr);
    else if (feof(fp))
        fputs("reached end of file\en", stderr);
    clearerr(fp);
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fopen (3),
.BR fread (3),
.BR fwrite (3),
.BR fgetc (3),
.BR fputc (3),
.BR ungetc (3),
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR feof (),
.BR ferror (),
and
.BR clearerr ()
functions conform to ISO C (C89 and later) and POSIX.1.
